  "statement": "A tiered liability framework is not a safety mechanism; it is a corporate insurance policy that codifies the power of incumbent labs by turning regulatory compliance into a barrier to entry. When we talk about these tiers, we are talking about a classic regulatory moat. Large firms like OpenAI or Google spend millions on lobbying to define what counts as 'high-risk,' effectively writing the rulebook so that only they have the capital and infrastructure to play by it. History shows this pattern clearly: during the 2002 Sarbanes-Oxley implementation, the compliance costs disproportionately crushed smaller firms, leaving the biggest players with even larger market shares. By mandating expensive, proprietary safety audits that only incumbents can afford to run, regulators aren't stopping risk; they are just clearing the field of competition.\n\nLiability frameworks are toothless when the underlying technology is a black box that defies traditional tort causation. You cannot sue a foundation model for negligence when the internal decision-making process is a proprietary, non-deterministic mess that even the developers don't fully understand. In the 1990s, the automotive industry used 'state-of-the-art' defense arguments to delay safety recalls for years, claiming their design was the best possible at the time. Today’s AI labs are doing the exact same thing, hiding behind the complexity of their training data to shield themselves from liability for systemic failures like biased financial lending or infrastructure disruption. If a model crashes a power grid in 2026, the 'state-of-the-art' defense will ensure the developer pays a fine that amounts to a rounding error on their quarterly balance sheet.\n\nWe need to stop treating AI as a magical, autonomous force and start treating it as industrial infrastructure. This is about water usage in Iowa, the $2-an-hour labor of content moderators in Nairobi, and the massive electricity draw on local grids. Any policy that focuses on abstract 'safety tiers' without addressing the physical reality of these systems is just theater. Who pays when a model causes a systemic failure in a regional hospital network? Right now, the answer is the public. Until we move from voluntary, industry-led audits to strict, public-utility-style liability where the developers are held directly responsible for the tangible, physical consequences of their products, these frameworks will remain nothing more than a way to keep the drawbridge pulled up.",
