  "statement": "The prevailing debate assumes that innovation and safety are competing goods, but this is a false dichotomy that ignores the structural requirements of critical infrastructure. We must transition from a model of voluntary self-regulation to a tiered liability framework where developers are legally accountable for the systemic impact of their foundation models. The Boeing 737 MAX tragedy serves as a permanent warning: when the Federal Aviation Administration delegated certification to the manufacturer, the resulting failure of oversight killed 346 people. We cannot afford to repeat this error by allowing private firms to conduct their own safety assessments for systems that underpin our power grids, financial markets, and communication networks.\n\nTiered liability functions as a mandatory guardrail rather than a bureaucratic hurdle. By tying legal and financial liability to the capability thresholds of a model, we force developers to internalize the cost of the risks they introduce into the public sphere. If a company deploys a model capable of autonomous vulnerability discovery, that company must hold the liability for any resulting infrastructure compromise. This does not stifle innovation; it directs capital toward the development of provably robust systems rather than reckless, high-speed scaling. Without this, we incentivize a race to the bottom where safety is discarded to secure a first-mover advantage.\n\nSystemic fragility is the inevitable outcome of interconnectivity without accountability. The 2021 Log4j vulnerability demonstrated how a single flaw in a foundational software component can expose millions of global systems to immediate, cascading failure. AI foundation models represent an even deeper layer of the digital stack, and their failure modes are far more complex and opaque than legacy software. If we do not impose independent, third-party auditing requirements on these models before they reach scale, we are effectively choosing to leave our civilizational foundations exposed to catastrophic, unmanaged error. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable; on what authority do we claim we can remove them now without inviting collapse?",
